<p>
Randy Ridenour has taken <a href="https://randyridenour.net/posts/2026-11-01-emacs-literate-calc-mode.html">another step in the journey so many of are on</a>: moving everything inside Emacs. Regular Irreal readers will recall that Ridenour is a Philosophy professor so he doesn’t have much need for high powered math tools but he <i>does</i> have to deal with grade calculations and various budget items. He had been using <a href="https://soulver.app/">Soulver</a> for this but, of course, this meant leaving Emacs, something all we Emacers hate.
</p>
<p>
Then he stumbled on <a href="https://github.com/sulami/literate-calc-mode.el">literate-calc-mode</a>. It did everything that Soulver did and Ridenour didn’t have to leave Emacs to use it. It’s been a win for him and he’s happy with it.
</p>
<p>
When I read Ridenour’s post, something seemed familiar. It turns out that I’d <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=9241">written about literate-calc-mode</a> six years ago. It had just been released and no one had any experience with it yet. I meant to follow up but, of course, life intervened and I never did. I’m glad to see that it’s still around and has lived up to its potential.
</p>
<p>
I’m tempted to install it but I’m reasonably proficient with Calc itself so I probably wouldn’t use it enough to make it worthwhile. Still, if Reverse Polish Notation is not your thing and you’d rather deal with normal infix notation, literate-calc-mode may be a good fit. It’s easy to try out so give it a spin if you’re interested.</p>
